  "body": "When in the [\"Attribute value unquoted\" state](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#attribute-value-(unquoted)-state), seeing an `=` character is a parse error. This means that markup like `<a href=http://example.com?foo=bar>a link</a>` is technically invalid HTML.\r\n\r\nThe point of making the *other* characters in that parse error group is to help catch things that indicate you've accidentally left the start tag unclosed and started a fresh tag or something, like `<div foo=bar<a href=http://example.com>...`. The `=` restriction is presumably for the case where you just didn't put a space between your attributes, like `<a id=foohref=bar>`, but that seems less likely to be a problem?\r\n\r\nIn general, I think `=` being a parse error is more likely to cause a spurious parse error than it is to catch an actual parse problem. We should possible remove that from the category and just let this be a normal character.\r\n\r\n(I caught this because I started flagging parse errors in Bikeshed. I'm going to relax this particular character because having unquoted urls is useful.)",
